#include <vector>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <random>
#include <chrono>

namespace RandomService {
    class Generator {
        private:
            static std::unique_ptr<Generator> instance;

            // Use the Mersenne Twister algorithm for generating random numbers
            std::mt19937 mt;

            // Vector to store the random numbers
            std::vector<double> randomNumbers;

            // Mutex and condition variable to handle concurrent requests
            std::mutex mutex;
            std::condition_variable cv;

        public:
            Generator() {
                mt.seed(std::chrono::system_clock::now().time_since_epoch().count());
            }

            static Generator* getInstance() {
                if (!instance) {
                    instance = std::make_unique<Generator>();
                }
                return instance.get();
            }

            // Method to generate a sequence of random numbers
            void generateSequence(size_t length, std::vector<double>& result) {
                // Lock the mutex and wait for the condition variable
                std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
                cv.wait(lock, [&]{ return randomNumbers.size() >= length; });

                // Generate the random numbers
                result = std::vector<double>(randomNumbers.begin(), randomNumbers.begin() + length);

                // Erase the generated random numbers from the vector
                randomNumbers.erase(randomNumbers.begin(), randomNumbers.begin() + length);

                // Notify all waiting threads that there is space in the vector again
                cv.notify_all();
            }
    };
}
```
This implementation uses a static singleton instance of the Generator class, which ensures that only one instance of the service is created and shared among all clients. The mutex and condition variable are used to handle concurrent requests and ensure thread safety. The `generateSequence` method first locks the mutex, then waits for the condition variable until there are enough random numbers in the vector to generate a sequence of length `length`. It then generates the random numbers, erases them from the vector, and notifies all waiting threads that there is space in the vector again.

This implementation should be optimized for both high throughput and low latency under heavy load by minimizing the time spent in the mutex and condition variable, as well as using a suitable data structure to store the random numbers. Additionally, it would be beneficial to integrate the service with an external data source for configuration settings, such as a configuration file or database, to ensure seamless updates without disrupting ongoing operations.']

