Here is an example of a C function that manages a dynamic list of LTE UE Contexts:
```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>

// Define the structure for a Measurement Report
struct MeasurementReport {
    int timestamp; // Timestamp of the measurement report
    float signal_strength; // Signal strength of the measurement report
};

// Define the structure for an LTE UE Context
struct LTEUEContext {
    int ue_id; // Unique ID for the UE
    int rnti; // RNTI (Random Network Transfer Entity) for the UE
    struct MeasurementReport *mr_list; // List of measurement reports for the UE
};

// Define the linked list structure for storing LTE UE Contexts
struct LTEUEContextList {
    struct LTEUEContext *head; // Head node of the list
    struct LTEUEContext *tail; // Tail node of the list
    size_t size; // Size of the list (number of elements)
};

// Initialize the linked list for storing LTE UE Contexts
void initLTEUEContextList(struct LTEUEContextList *list) {
    list->head = NULL;
    list->tail = NULL;
    list->size = 0;
}

// Insert a new LTE UE Context into the linked list
void insertLTEUEContext(struct LTEUEContextList *list, struct LTEUEContext *ue_context) {
    // Check if the list is empty
    if (list->head == NULL) {
        list->head = ue_context;
        list->tail = ue_context;
    } else {
        // Add the new UE Context to the tail of the list
        list->tail->next = ue_context;
        list->tail = ue_context;
    }
    list->size++;
}

// Remove an LTE UE Context from the linked list
void removeLTEUEContext(struct LTEUEContextList *list, struct LTEUEContext *ue_context) {
    // Check if the list is empty
    if (list->head == NULL) {
        return;
    }
    // Find the node in the list that contains the UE Context
    struct LTEUEContext *node = list->head;
    while (node != NULL && node->next != ue_context) {
        node = node->next;
    }
    // Remove the node from the list
    if (node == NULL) {
        return;
    } else {
        node->next = node->next->next;
        if (list->tail == ue_context) {
            list->tail = node;
        }
    }
    list->size--;
}

// Search for an LTE UE Context in the linked list by its unique ID
struct LTEUEContext *searchLTEUEContext(struct LTEUEContextList *list, int ue_id) {
    // Check if the list is empty
    if (list->head == NULL) {
        return NULL;
    }
    // Find the node in the list that contains the UE Context
    struct LTEUEContext *node = list->head;
    while (node != NULL && node->ue_id != ue_id) {
        node = node->next;
    }
    return node;
}

// Update the measurement reports for an LTE UE Context in the linked list
void updateMeasurementReports(struct LTEUEContextList *list, struct LTEUEContext *ue_context, int timestamp, float signal_strength) {
    // Check if the list is empty
    if (list->head == NULL) {
        return;
    }
    // Find the node in the list that contains the UE Context
    struct LTEUEContext *node = searchLTEUEContext(list, ue_context->ue_id);
    if (node != NULL) {
        // Add a new measurement report to the list
        struct MeasurementReport *mr = malloc(sizeof(struct MeasurementReport));
        mr->timestamp = timestamp;
        mr->signal_strength = signal_strength;
        node->mr_list = mr;
    } else {
        // Add a new UE Context to the list
        struct LTEUEContext *new_ue_context = malloc(sizeof(struct LTEUEContext));
        new_ue_context->ue_id = ue_context->ue_id;
        new_ue_context->rnti = ue_context->rnti;
        insertLTEUEContext(list, new_ue_context);
    }
}

// Free the memory used by a linked list of LTE UE Contexts
void freeLTEUEContextList(struct LTEUEContextList *list) {
    // Check if the list is empty
    if (list->head == NULL) {
        return;
    }
    // Iterate through the list and free the memory for each node
    struct LTEUEContext *node = list->head;
    while (node != NULL) {
        struct LTEUEContext *next_node = node->next;
        free(node);
        node = next_node;
    }
}
```
This function uses a custom linked list structure optimized for insertions and deletions under high-load conditions. The linked list is implemented using a `struct LTEUEContextList` that contains the head, tail, and size of the list. Each node in the list contains an `int ue_id`, an `int rnti`, and a `struct MeasurementReport *mr_list`.

The function `initLTEUEContextList` initializes the linked list by setting the head and tail nodes to NULL and the size of the list to 0.

The function `insertLTEUEContext` inserts a new LTE UE Context into the linked list by adding it to the tail node. If the list is empty, both the head and tail nodes are set to the new UE Context.

The function `removeLTEUEContext` removes an LTE UE Context from the linked list by finding the node in the list that contains the UE Context and removing it from the list.

The function `searchLTEUEContext` searches for an LTE UE Context in the linked list by its unique ID. If the list is empty or the UE Context is not found, NULL is returned. Otherwise, the node containing the UE Context is returned.

The function `updateMeasurementReports` updates the measurement reports for an LTE UE Context in the linked list by adding a new measurement report to the list if it is not already present. If the UE Context is not found in the list, it is added as a new node to the end of the list.

The function `freeLTEUEContextList` frees the memory used by the linked list of LTE UE Contexts by iterating through the list and freeing the memory for each node.

To balance the choice between a simpler but less efficient data structure and a more complex one with better performance under stress, developers should consider using a combination of both approaches. For example, they could use a simple linked list for storing LTE UE Contexts in most cases, but switch to a more complex data structure optimized for insertions and deletions under high-load conditions when necessary.

To maintain data integrity and minimize latency, developers should use synchronization mechanisms such as mutexes or semaphores to ensure that multiple threads accessing the linked list from concurrently do not interfere with each other. They could also use techniques such as copy-on-write or snapshotting to reduce the impact of contention on the data structure.']

